00:03:18.910 --> 00:03:21.190
I'd love to introduce
both Joe Powers,

00:03:21.190 --> 00:03:25.450
principal data scientist at
Intuit, and Derek Beaton,

00:03:25.450 --> 00:03:28.585
director of advanced analytics
at Saint Michael's Hospital.

00:03:29.440 --> 00:03:32.170
Do you both want to maybe
kick things off by just sharing a

00:03:32.170 --> 00:03:35.200
little bit with us about each
of your roles and kind of say

00:03:35.200 --> 00:03:37.990
hello before we dive into ROI?

00:03:37.990 --> 00:03:39.580
- Sure, Derek, you
want to lead off?

00:03:39.580 --> 00:03:40.810
- Sure, thanks.

00:03:41.800 --> 00:03:43.960
So I'm Derek, director
of advanced analytics

00:03:44.890 --> 00:03:47.470
in a unit at St. Michael's
Hospital in Toronto.

00:03:48.340 --> 00:03:50.830
Our unit is data science
and advanced analytics.

00:03:50.830 --> 00:03:52.345
We are an applied
health care unit.

00:03:53.120 --> 00:03:53.740
So we put

00:03:55.210 --> 00:03:59.860
stats and now AI tools
into production for mostly clinical use.

00:03:59.860 --> 00:04:02.620
But there are quite a few
operational or like staffing

00:04:02.620 --> 00:04:04.735
solutions that we
put into place.

00:04:05.830 --> 00:04:07.240
We're not research.

00:04:07.240 --> 00:04:09.865
It's all about getting things actually
done and into the hands of users.

00:04:12.120 --> 00:04:13.875
Overall, our group
is about 30 people.

00:04:14.580 --> 00:04:18.329
We technically work
across three hospitals--

00:04:18.329 --> 00:04:20.700
St. Michael's, St.
Joseph's, and Providence,

00:04:20.700 --> 00:04:23.010
all in Toronto as part of
the Unity Health Network.

00:04:24.120 --> 00:04:26.100
We are mostly in
our house. We have

00:04:27.770 --> 00:04:30.020
maybe 70% of our
solutions in are.

00:04:30.020 --> 00:04:32.270
And then we've got a
variety of things in Python,

00:04:32.270 --> 00:04:33.980
including some new stuff
we've recently launched

00:04:35.420 --> 00:04:38.352
with a little bit of
stuff in, pretty much any

00:04:39.500 --> 00:04:43.070
sort of analytics, AI and
ML domain. So imaging,

00:04:43.070 --> 00:04:47.270
clinical prediction, patient flow,
forecasting, you name it,

00:04:47.270 --> 00:04:48.890
we probably do a
little bit of it.

00:04:49.642 --> 00:04:51.235
- Awesome. Joe, you
want to go next?

00:04:51.235 --> 00:04:52.240
JOE: Yeah, sure.

00:04:52.990 --> 00:04:55.510
So, yeah, I'm Joe
Powers. I work at Intuit.

00:04:55.510 --> 00:04:57.940
You're probably familiar with
some of our flagship products

00:04:57.940 --> 00:05:00.250
like QuickBooks,
TurboTax, Mailchimp.

00:05:01.750 --> 00:05:04.720
And, yeah, so
I lead experimentation, which

00:05:05.320 --> 00:05:06.910
is a huge amount of ROI

00:05:08.110 --> 00:05:09.895
involved in it, largely around

00:05:11.280 --> 00:05:14.310
investigating new methods for how
we could run faster and

00:05:14.310 --> 00:05:18.930
more accurate AB tests
for product and marketing decisions.

00:05:18.930 --> 00:05:21.390
And so when you move

00:05:22.170 --> 00:05:23.965
the whole company, you're

00:05:26.190 --> 00:05:28.785
shifting their decision-making process
to a new statistical method.

00:05:29.400 --> 00:05:30.720
People want to know. Like

00:05:31.860 --> 00:05:35.295
why is the juice going to be
worth the squeeze on this? And so,

00:05:36.090 --> 00:05:38.280
yeah, when Rachael
brought this topic up,

00:05:38.280 --> 00:05:39.540
I was I was happy to join in.

00:05:39.540 --> 00:05:40.040
Because

00:05:40.830 --> 00:05:43.110
I feel like it's one
of those hidden curricula topics that

00:05:43.110 --> 00:05:47.010
like it's central to doing your
job as a data scientist. But

00:05:47.010 --> 00:05:51.688
absolutely no one
teaches this. So, good

00:05:53.480 --> 00:05:54.515
for you for being here today.

00:05:57.290 --> 00:05:59.120
- So I was thinking we jump

00:06:00.020 --> 00:06:03.380
in and get started with sharing
a little bit about how you both

00:06:03.380 --> 00:06:04.370
have approached this.

00:06:04.370 --> 00:06:08.675
And then people can jump
in with questions and thoughts, too.

00:06:09.530 --> 00:06:12.710
I know you both have
very different approaches to this, too.

00:06:12.710 --> 00:06:16.220
And it's really cool to see
the difference across industries as well.

00:06:17.840 --> 00:06:21.455
I don't know who wants to
get started. But I was thinking maybe

00:06:22.280 --> 00:06:24.700
kind of giving us a baseline
and sharing a little bit about

00:06:24.700 --> 00:06:25.940
what you do.

00:06:28.600 --> 00:06:28.990
Derek,

00:06:28.990 --> 00:06:32.410
do you want to maybe start
with explaining how the process your

00:06:32.410 --> 00:06:33.340
teams rolled out?

00:06:33.340 --> 00:06:34.480
- Sure, yeah.

00:06:35.350 --> 00:06:37.700
And in some ways, maybe
this will feel really specific.

00:06:38.380 --> 00:06:38.880
But

00:06:39.610 --> 00:06:41.500
it's because we're in
health care in a hospital.

00:06:41.500 --> 00:06:43.900
But I think a lot of this
is pretty generalizable. So,

00:06:44.740 --> 00:06:47.200
and I'll share a document or part
of a document with us in a

00:06:47.200 --> 00:06:48.535
moment. So when

00:06:49.510 --> 00:06:50.860
a project comes our way,

00:06:51.820 --> 00:06:54.440
it usually comes from
someone on the ground,

00:06:54.440 --> 00:06:59.770
so a physician or someone in HR
or say a clinical manager in some way.

00:07:00.310 --> 00:07:02.275
They have a problem, they
know data can solve it.

00:07:02.800 --> 00:07:05.200
And they work with a
few of us in the team,

00:07:05.200 --> 00:07:06.190
primarily the directors

00:07:07.300 --> 00:07:11.890
and the product managers
on filling out and detailing

00:07:11.890 --> 00:07:13.210
project intake form.

00:07:13.210 --> 00:07:15.520
And one of the most
critical parts of the project intake

00:07:15.520 --> 00:07:16.870
form, which I will--

00:07:17.900 --> 00:07:19.130
Rachael, am able to share?

00:07:19.890 --> 00:07:25.040
- Yes, I will just make
you a co-host so that you're able

00:07:25.040 --> 00:07:28.230
to. And I got rid
of my Posit logo,

00:07:28.230 --> 00:07:30.320
which was backwards in
the background. And now, I'm in

00:07:30.320 --> 00:07:33.890
a fancy boardroom here for this
ROI conversation.

00:07:33.890 --> 00:07:35.200
DEREK: Awesome.

00:07:36.200 --> 00:07:39.890
- One second. I'll make
you co-host. There you go.

00:07:39.890 --> 00:07:42.820
- Awesome, thank you. So
I'll just do this real fast,

00:07:42.820 --> 00:07:45.440
and you can take away my
co-host abilities in a moment.

00:07:45.440 --> 00:07:46.680
RACHAEL: You can keep them.

00:07:46.680 --> 00:07:49.340
I just didn't want everybody
to be able to screen share.

00:07:49.340 --> 00:07:49.970
- So

00:07:50.900 --> 00:07:52.940
as part of that project intake,

00:07:52.940 --> 00:07:55.610
there's a key section
here called evaluation metrics.

00:07:55.610 --> 00:07:56.510
And right off the bat, you

00:07:57.380 --> 00:07:59.450
should have a good idea
of whether you're trying to

00:07:59.450 --> 00:08:02.870
address mortality, admission,
length of stay, financial

00:08:02.870 --> 00:08:04.730
issues, or human
effort, generally,

00:08:04.730 --> 00:08:06.890
across the types of problems
you want to solve in the

00:08:06.890 --> 00:08:08.820
hospital. There's
another box, other,

00:08:08.820 --> 00:08:11.390
which is usually an indicator
of like we probably aren't

00:08:11.390 --> 00:08:13.670
going to take this on or it's
hard to define or hard to measure.

00:08:14.420 --> 00:08:17.660
But a lot of these are
generally easier to track or

00:08:17.660 --> 00:08:20.120
measure and assess the impact.

00:08:20.120 --> 00:08:21.275
And from there,

00:08:21.950 --> 00:08:26.720
we work with our collaborators on
how to define the primary metric.

00:08:26.720 --> 00:08:29.810
So are you trying to do
something to reduce a length of stay?

00:08:29.810 --> 00:08:33.350
Then we are going to look
at how to do that as well as

00:08:33.350 --> 00:08:38.705
tracking the actual length of
stay pre and post deployment. And

00:08:40.500 --> 00:08:41.320
when we do this,

00:08:41.320 --> 00:08:45.030
we have discussions early on
about what the expected level

00:08:45.030 --> 00:08:46.220
of change is.

00:08:46.720 --> 00:08:49.080
So if you think you can bring
length of stay down for,

00:08:50.560 --> 00:08:52.890
say, all patients
in a certain unit by

00:08:55.200 --> 00:08:57.030
let's say, 30 minutes
in an ICU or something.

00:08:57.030 --> 00:08:59.970
So you bring the overall
down quite a bit.

00:08:59.970 --> 00:09:01.860
We'll get this
written down here,

00:09:01.860 --> 00:09:04.335
and that's how we proceed
and measure and assess things

00:09:05.820 --> 00:09:06.915
when it's after the fact.

00:09:07.530 --> 00:09:09.060
I'll stop sharing now.

00:09:09.060 --> 00:09:12.420
But we have a fairly
deep and detailed discussions at this

00:09:12.420 --> 00:09:15.060
intake stage about
what the metrics are,

00:09:15.060 --> 00:09:17.550
how we're going to measure it,
how we're going to track it,

00:09:17.550 --> 00:09:23.160
and what the level of change
is expected to be. And a lot

00:09:23.160 --> 00:09:26.925
of the expectation will come from
knowing the hospital or the system

00:09:27.660 --> 00:09:28.680
in general,

00:09:28.680 --> 00:09:31.920
knowing the types of changes
that do exist in other sites

00:09:31.920 --> 00:09:34.230
where you can point to
literature or something else

00:09:34.230 --> 00:09:36.640
somewhere else. And say look,
they saw a 10% reduction,

00:09:36.640 --> 00:09:39.570
so we would expect
about the same.

00:09:39.570 --> 00:09:43.420
So a lot of this is based
off of targeting something,

00:09:43.420 --> 00:09:48.730
identify a key health economics
metric, and let's work

00:09:48.730 --> 00:09:51.670
out how much we think we're
going to actually make a

00:09:51.670 --> 00:09:56.650
difference. If things aren't say
a huge benefit or if they

00:09:56.650 --> 00:09:59.020
fall in that other box,
this is where we say like,

00:09:59.020 --> 00:10:01.480
maybe we're not going to take
this on or we have to dig

00:10:01.480 --> 00:10:05.710
really deep into the types
of things that might yield value

00:10:05.710 --> 00:10:09.700
overall for either the unit,
the hospital, the system,

00:10:09.700 --> 00:10:10.675
like the whole network of ours,

00:10:11.650 --> 00:10:13.340
before considering
taking it on.

00:10:13.340 --> 00:10:16.690
So we get pretty deep into
this on how we actually

00:10:19.000 --> 00:10:21.940
assess and decide what to
do before we fully commit.

00:10:23.200 --> 00:10:26.935
As part of this where we
won't necessarily like--

00:10:28.150 --> 00:10:29.530
Rachael, so I guess
to one of your points,

00:10:29.530 --> 00:10:33.040
like before any commitment. There
is some commitment we have

00:10:35.290 --> 00:10:36.865
or that we'll get into. So

00:10:37.660 --> 00:10:39.730
if people come to us,
and they're like, yeah,

00:10:39.730 --> 00:10:42.550
I think we can get this
measure here, here, here,

00:10:42.550 --> 00:10:44.560
and we want to get a baseline.

00:10:44.560 --> 00:10:48.490
We'll absolutely work with anyone
to establish what those baselines are.

00:10:49.270 --> 00:10:51.760
So that part comes with
the project intake.

00:10:51.760 --> 00:10:56.500
It's just the deciding to
move into the full development of

00:10:56.500 --> 00:10:59.500
something is really where we
need to see what that baseline

00:10:59.500 --> 00:11:03.655
is and really dig into what
these improvements are going to be.

00:11:03.655 --> 00:11:05.710
- And I'm sorry if
you said this already,

00:11:05.710 --> 00:11:09.580
but how long have you had that
process in place with the intake form?

00:11:10.580 --> 00:11:11.740
- Quite a while.

00:11:12.430 --> 00:11:16.930
The intake form here has been
revised as recently as a little

00:11:16.930 --> 00:11:18.220
over a year ago.

00:11:18.220 --> 00:11:21.620
But those metrics have been
in place pretty much since

00:11:23.330 --> 00:11:25.340
the start of this group,
which predates me.

00:11:26.210 --> 00:11:29.885
So always trying to target
big health economics type metrics.

00:11:30.560 --> 00:11:34.820
- Well I was just asking in
the chat. I was curious for people

00:11:34.820 --> 00:11:37.880
listening in if you
have something similar to that,

00:11:37.880 --> 00:11:39.380
like a project intake form.

00:11:40.250 --> 00:11:41.480
And if so,

00:11:41.480 --> 00:11:44.120
what some of those evaluation
metrics might be for you.

00:11:46.610 --> 00:11:49.350
Maybe a good reminder, I
should have said up front.

00:11:49.350 --> 00:11:53.420
But I love to see everybody
talking in the chat and during

00:11:53.420 --> 00:11:54.470
these sessions, too.

00:11:54.470 --> 00:11:56.960
If you've been to
a Data Science Hangout before,

00:11:56.960 --> 00:11:59.750
you might see a lot of
resources get shared in the

00:11:59.750 --> 00:12:02.105
chat. So feel free to use that

00:12:02.780 --> 00:12:04.085
during the session, too.

00:12:05.460 --> 00:12:06.870
Joe, do you want to
share a little bit--

00:12:06.870 --> 00:12:07.380
JOE: Yeah, sure.

00:12:07.380 --> 00:12:08.834
- --with us about what you do?

00:12:08.834 --> 00:12:09.276
-

00:12:09.276 --> 00:12:11.970
I think just kind of echo some
key things that Derek said

00:12:11.970 --> 00:12:15.330
around like identifying
those key metrics.

00:12:16.620 --> 00:12:18.000
A lot of people talk
about stakeholders.

00:12:18.000 --> 00:12:19.470
I tend to think
about gatekeepers.

00:12:19.470 --> 00:12:21.030
You have lots of
stakeholders, but really,

00:12:21.030 --> 00:12:23.250
thinking now about which ones
are the gatekeepers that are

00:12:23.250 --> 00:12:24.525
going to block or allow

00:12:25.500 --> 00:12:27.100
your project to roll out.

00:12:27.100 --> 00:12:29.490
So like what are the
metrics that they really care about?

00:12:29.490 --> 00:12:32.480
And yeah, establishing
baseline. There's

00:12:33.300 --> 00:12:36.120
very likely a dashboard
that those leaders are regularly

00:12:36.120 --> 00:12:39.465
referring to that you can
anchor to as a shared reality.

00:12:40.470 --> 00:12:44.130
And then what is a
reasonable lift that you could expect?

00:12:45.000 --> 00:12:47.850
That could be based
on literature review.

00:12:47.850 --> 00:12:50.130
It could be
based on qualitative testing.

00:12:50.130 --> 00:12:54.510
If you looked at 20 users in
UX settings, and you said, oh, our

00:12:54.510 --> 00:12:58.260
invoice completion rate went from
20% to 90% with the new

00:12:59.400 --> 00:13:00.060
invoice flow.

00:13:01.350 --> 00:13:05.170
That is actually a great anchor
point that you could now start

00:13:05.170 --> 00:13:08.350
thinking about. Like, OK,
that's in an idealized setting.

00:13:08.350 --> 00:13:09.820
But when it moves into
the product, like,

00:13:09.820 --> 00:13:11.410
what's reasonable to expect?

00:13:13.150 --> 00:13:14.230
So a lot of times,

00:13:14.230 --> 00:13:16.640
you're just dealing with
the best data or best

00:13:17.590 --> 00:13:19.450
information you can
get your hands on.

00:13:19.450 --> 00:13:21.830
And that can run
the gamut from maybe

00:13:22.810 --> 00:13:26.230
it was captured in your data
lake to running a few surveys

00:13:26.230 --> 00:13:29.110
or doing some qualitative
sessions. But any

00:13:29.890 --> 00:13:31.075
information is
better than none.

00:13:32.080 --> 00:13:35.950
So, yeah, key metrics,
baselines, reasonable lift. And

00:13:37.420 --> 00:13:39.550
then, actually,
that's where for me,

00:13:39.550 --> 00:13:41.950
it becomes a data
simulation task after that.

00:13:41.950 --> 00:13:44.560
And I'm happy to share a
little bit because like I said,

00:13:44.560 --> 00:13:46.510
nobody ever taught
me how to do this,

00:13:46.510 --> 00:13:48.850
but it's just
become like my go-to

00:13:49.570 --> 00:13:50.150
for the workflow.

00:13:52.030 --> 00:13:52.530
So

00:13:53.350 --> 00:13:56.830
I'm in our studio right
now, and I've loaded the tidyverse.

00:13:56.830 --> 00:13:59.260
And so you can imagine, yeah,
my baseline is this rate A.

00:13:59.260 --> 00:14:00.235
So maybe I have a 50%

00:14:01.060 --> 00:14:05.320
rate. Maybe this is like
a conversion rate or a click

00:14:05.320 --> 00:14:08.980
rate, whatever is that key
metric for your gatekeepers. And

00:14:09.820 --> 00:14:11.650
maybe I'm just going to explore
some like reasonable lift.

00:14:11.650 --> 00:14:13.810
Like maybe I think I could lift
this a tenth of a percent with

00:14:13.810 --> 00:14:14.500
some new program.

00:14:15.430 --> 00:14:19.900
OK, I've got 100,000
customers, and I'm going to run

00:14:19.900 --> 00:14:22.070
4,000 simulations. So,

00:14:23.260 --> 00:14:26.300
yeah, I can execute
this now in this cell.

00:14:26.300 --> 00:14:28.750
So I'm using the
binomial distribution.

00:14:28.750 --> 00:14:31.360
If you're not familiar or
you're not comfortable using

00:14:31.360 --> 00:14:33.390
these likelihood functions,

00:14:34.720 --> 00:14:38.095
I think this is something
just really worth leaning into. Like

00:14:39.720 --> 00:14:42.780
a lot of abstract
statistics become really concrete and

00:14:42.780 --> 00:14:45.300
intuitive once you get
comfortable simulating data

00:14:45.300 --> 00:14:46.560
with likelihood functions.

00:14:47.070 --> 00:14:49.560
So all I'm doing here is I'm
running 4,000 simulations

00:14:50.250 --> 00:14:54.450
with 100,000 trials. And I
can set the probability of

00:14:54.450 --> 00:14:55.470
success on each trial.

00:14:56.510 --> 00:14:57.770
And this is going to output

00:14:58.490 --> 00:15:00.350
like a tally of events.

00:15:00.350 --> 00:15:02.750
But then once I divide
that by the population,

00:15:02.750 --> 00:15:05.045
I'll always get a rate.
So maybe this outputs

00:15:06.950 --> 00:15:08.730
50,000 events.

00:15:09.530 --> 00:15:14.090
But when that divides by 100,000,
now, I get a 50% rate. OK.

00:15:14.090 --> 00:15:14.590
So

00:15:16.130 --> 00:15:18.170
I could actually just
give you a peek at-- give

00:15:18.170 --> 00:15:20.210
you a sense of
[INAUDIBLE].

00:15:22.780 --> 00:15:25.680
So, yeah, for my first
simulation, I have 50.1%.

00:15:27.450 --> 00:15:28.447
The second one, 50.2.

00:15:29.640 --> 00:15:32.685
I hope this is getting clearer.
I tried to keep this minimal.

00:15:32.685 --> 00:15:33.660
- Would you--

00:15:33.660 --> 00:15:37.293
like this now, would you
be able to describe what's

00:15:38.260 --> 00:15:41.805
a project that you might be working
on where you would be doing this?

00:15:42.350 --> 00:15:42.990
- So

00:15:44.780 --> 00:15:45.680
you could think of--

00:15:45.680 --> 00:15:48.830
this is really very close to
the idea of a power analysis.

00:15:48.830 --> 00:15:53.120
If you were introducing
this 0.1% improvement, you could

00:15:53.120 --> 00:15:56.240
start asking questions like, how
likely are we to detect it?

00:15:57.350 --> 00:15:57.860
And so,

00:15:59.020 --> 00:16:02.605
even in this case, if I just
look at how often are my

00:16:03.220 --> 00:16:06.010
B simulations bigger
than my A simulations?

00:16:06.010 --> 00:16:07.990
That's not saying it's
statistically significant.

00:16:07.990 --> 00:16:09.295
It's just saying bigger at all.

00:16:10.240 --> 00:16:15.175
It's this small of a change
100,000 users. 68% of the time,

00:16:16.120 --> 00:16:17.560
it would it larger.

00:16:18.850 --> 00:16:21.400
And you could explore like
the range of differences that you

00:16:21.400 --> 00:16:22.705
might observe like

00:16:24.760 --> 00:16:27.820
a 95% interval
of those potential differences.

00:16:28.690 --> 00:16:33.040
The very concrete examples that I
could give are like when I'm exploring

00:16:33.820 --> 00:16:35.485
a new AB testing method.

00:16:36.780 --> 00:16:40.320
What I'm going to do is I'm
going to simulate the test data from

00:16:41.100 --> 00:16:42.660
100,000 AB tests.

00:16:42.660 --> 00:16:45.660
And then I can start
asking very practical questions from

00:16:45.660 --> 00:16:46.710
those simulated data.

00:16:46.710 --> 00:16:49.290
Like how often did I detect

00:16:50.010 --> 00:16:51.300
the superior

00:16:52.140 --> 00:16:53.310
treatment condition?

00:16:53.310 --> 00:16:57.630
How long did it take
to detect the superior treatment condition?

00:16:57.630 --> 00:17:01.440
How many additional conversions
did we yield because we

00:17:01.440 --> 00:17:04.784
detected the better experience
earlier and rolled it out

00:17:05.640 --> 00:17:08.219
to 100% earlier in the season?

00:17:09.089 --> 00:17:13.890
Those are the kind of return
on investment questions that I can

00:17:13.890 --> 00:17:18.839
explore using very basic
data simulation that my

00:17:18.839 --> 00:17:21.570
gatekeepers, my stakeholders
really care about.

00:17:22.140 --> 00:17:25.990
They want to know before
they greenlight using

00:17:26.910 --> 00:17:28.710
a new statistical
method in season.

00:17:28.710 --> 00:17:29.550
They're going to want to know,

00:17:29.550 --> 00:17:31.770
does this detect benefit
more often and faster?

00:17:31.770 --> 00:17:34.245
Does it detect harm
more often and faster?

00:17:35.880 --> 00:17:36.380
And

00:17:38.110 --> 00:17:38.845
this is all just--

00:17:39.430 --> 00:17:41.680
that initial return
on investment research,

00:17:41.680 --> 00:17:43.420
they're going to want to the
same thing on the tail end of

00:17:43.420 --> 00:17:46.736
the project about how
well this lived up to

00:17:48.640 --> 00:17:49.630
your projections.

00:17:49.630 --> 00:17:54.700
But I just wanted to show
this as using these likelihood functions.

00:17:55.660 --> 00:17:57.775
In NumPy, you have
available in Base-R.

00:17:58.810 --> 00:18:02.080
But they're very powerful
and very persuasive when you're

00:18:02.080 --> 00:18:05.140
trying to do like ROI
sizing for a new project.

00:18:05.910 --> 00:18:07.790
RACHAEL: Oh, that's
great. Thank you.

00:18:08.930 --> 00:18:09.430
And

00:18:10.070 --> 00:18:11.150
thinking about that,

00:18:11.750 --> 00:18:14.840
the ROI that you showed
there, how do you

00:18:15.560 --> 00:18:18.560
translate that into
communication with the business

00:18:18.560 --> 00:18:22.205
stakeholders? Is it through
like a business case document?

00:18:25.186 --> 00:18:29.190
- Through all
means possible. So it's,

00:18:31.420 --> 00:18:31.920
again,

00:18:33.220 --> 00:18:34.420
as I mentioned,
gatekeepers earlier.

00:18:34.420 --> 00:18:36.340
Like thinking about
who's approval? Who's buying,

00:18:36.340 --> 00:18:38.290
and you need for a
project to get greenlit?

00:18:40.800 --> 00:18:42.930
That person is going to have
a lot of people demanding their

00:18:42.930 --> 00:18:45.810
attention. They've got a lot
of fires to put out. Like,

00:18:45.810 --> 00:18:49.875
how are you going to get your idea
in front of them so that it sticks?

00:18:51.210 --> 00:18:53.370
You give a 20-minute
presentation.

00:18:54.000 --> 00:18:55.890
20 minutes later, they're
probably only going to--

00:18:55.890 --> 00:18:57.030
if you succeeded,

00:18:57.030 --> 00:18:59.100
they're only going to remember
one or two things from that

00:18:59.100 --> 00:19:04.080
presentation. And so the
clarity of your communication--

00:19:04.080 --> 00:19:06.225
and we had a former CEO
who loved the expression,

00:19:06.225 --> 00:19:09.040
"Repetition doesn't
sully the prayer." Just

00:19:09.840 --> 00:19:11.850
repeat, repeat, repeat.

00:19:11.850 --> 00:19:15.780
And just keep driving home
that central message that appeals to

00:19:15.780 --> 00:19:17.940
them, like if they
have an urgent problem,

00:19:17.940 --> 00:19:20.460
and you're offering the
solution, if you're doing it

00:19:20.460 --> 00:19:22.305
clearly, you're not going to have
to offer it that many times.

00:19:23.340 --> 00:19:24.615
But be ready to deliver.

00:19:25.440 --> 00:19:25.940
And

00:19:26.460 --> 00:19:28.200
so, anyhow, but yeah,
through communication,

00:19:28.200 --> 00:19:31.740
it's like start sending word
out in Slack channel, offer

00:19:32.460 --> 00:19:36.990
I guess more peer-supportive
trainings through Zoom meetings

00:19:36.990 --> 00:19:38.940
and live meetings, get
your peers on board,

00:19:40.540 --> 00:19:41.755
work through your
manager to get

00:19:42.490 --> 00:19:44.230
face time in--

00:19:44.230 --> 00:19:46.570
there's usually a recurring
executive presentations,

00:19:47.260 --> 00:19:50.350
where you can get in
front of your leaders to pitch

00:19:51.580 --> 00:19:52.780
your potential solutions.

00:19:52.780 --> 00:19:54.460
And if you're coming in there,

00:19:54.460 --> 00:19:58.120
not only with the
performance of your solution,

00:19:58.120 --> 00:20:02.200
but the return on investment in
a metric they care about and recognize,

00:20:02.200 --> 00:20:05.050
like this is just all stuff
that greases the skids to make

00:20:05.050 --> 00:20:08.875
sure that your project gets
a mandate and goes into practice.

00:20:11.460 --> 00:20:12.300
And like we said earlier,

00:20:12.300 --> 00:20:15.000
just be ready on the tail
end to verify that you delivered

00:20:15.000 --> 00:20:16.200
what you promised. Because

00:20:17.520 --> 00:20:21.960
that really kind of like puts
a bow on it and really cements

00:20:21.960 --> 00:20:24.660
the relationship that in the
future it's just going to be

00:20:24.660 --> 00:20:28.050
that much easier for you
to get that face time,

00:20:28.050 --> 00:20:30.390
to get the approval to put
your projects into production.

00:20:31.190 --> 00:20:32.490
- Definitely.

00:20:33.120 --> 00:20:36.010
George, you asked a question
in the chat a bit earlier.

00:20:36.010 --> 00:20:36.915
Do you to jump in here?

00:20:38.436 --> 00:20:39.670
GEORGE: OK, yeah.

00:20:40.660 --> 00:20:43.030
Sorry, I'm battling a cold,
and my voice is pretty terrible.

00:20:44.830 --> 00:20:49.420
Yeah, I was wondering how
you would estimate or measure your

00:20:49.420 --> 00:20:51.130
own time investment.

00:20:51.130 --> 00:20:53.754
Because I think the other
half of this equation is

00:20:55.370 --> 00:20:58.670
understanding how much effort
it will take for you to

00:20:59.300 --> 00:21:02.510
do that. And also, how
accurate those estimates are?

00:21:04.256 --> 00:21:06.765
- I have only seen that

00:21:08.670 --> 00:21:10.755
"accurately" measured
with survey data.

00:21:12.930 --> 00:21:15.960
I've tried to use calendar data
for these kind of projects.

00:21:15.960 --> 00:21:17.580
It is meaningless.

00:21:18.180 --> 00:21:21.510
The loyalty of people to
their calendars, the accuracy,

00:21:21.510 --> 00:21:24.570
the coding, just don't even
try it would be my advice.

00:21:25.920 --> 00:21:28.980
But just asking like a good
survey question about in

00:21:30.660 --> 00:21:31.350
the past week--

00:21:31.350 --> 00:21:33.360
like surveying
analysts, for instance,

00:21:33.360 --> 00:21:35.010
or surveying the key people.

00:21:35.010 --> 00:21:35.820
Like in the past week,

00:21:35.820 --> 00:21:38.550
how much time did you spend
on like these parts of your work?

00:21:39.900 --> 00:21:42.510
Is that a typical week?
But usually it helps to anchor.

00:21:42.510 --> 00:21:45.060
Like anchoring surveys to a
specific time period can really

00:21:45.060 --> 00:21:48.000
improve the accuracy
of responses. But honestly,

00:21:48.000 --> 00:21:50.370
just survey data
is the only thing

00:21:52.320 --> 00:21:54.330
to answer your question that
I think that's the best signal.

00:21:54.330 --> 00:21:57.120
It's not great, but I think
it's the best you can do.

00:21:58.530 --> 00:22:00.875
- Derek, do you want
to add anything there?

00:22:00.875 --> 00:22:02.900
- Yeah, so estimating
ahead of time,

00:22:04.280 --> 00:22:04.805
we don't.

00:22:05.780 --> 00:22:06.980
It's really hard
to make a guess,

00:22:06.980 --> 00:22:09.140
especially when a
lot of stuff we do is

00:22:10.190 --> 00:22:12.170
pretty variable and unknown,
especially in the early start--

00:22:12.170 --> 00:22:13.130
like parts of the projects.

00:22:14.060 --> 00:22:15.440
Measuring later,

00:22:15.440 --> 00:22:18.020
we're probably a little bit
better about once we're like

00:22:18.020 --> 00:22:18.980
really in the weeds.

00:22:18.980 --> 00:22:21.920
And this would come a lot
from our product managers and the

00:22:21.920 --> 00:22:24.155
way we've set up
our sprint cycles.

00:22:25.610 --> 00:22:27.560
We got a pretty good track of--

00:22:27.560 --> 00:22:28.355
or at least within

00:22:29.330 --> 00:22:30.590
good like small windows.

00:22:30.590 --> 00:22:33.830
We have a pretty good sense
of what we're doing and how much

00:22:33.830 --> 00:22:35.680
effort we're putting into
the respective projects.

00:22:36.950 --> 00:22:38.525
But that's definitely
not something we--

00:22:40.340 --> 00:22:45.050
I would say estimate very far
in advance, so like at the ROI

00:22:45.050 --> 00:22:46.430
project intake stage,

00:22:46.430 --> 00:22:50.120
more so like in the project is this
going to take you a week, two weeks,

00:22:50.720 --> 00:22:52.160
one day stuff like that.

00:22:52.160 --> 00:22:52.660
But

00:22:53.990 --> 00:22:55.460
I think where maybe
that question,

00:22:56.730 --> 00:22:58.110
if I were to make
a guess, George,

00:22:59.220 --> 00:23:00.675
is this about trade off?

00:23:01.200 --> 00:23:03.690
Like how many person hours
is it really going to take?

00:23:03.690 --> 00:23:04.780
Is it going to take three,

00:23:04.780 --> 00:23:08.370
four years for something to
happen and maybe that cancels

00:23:08.370 --> 00:23:09.885
out some of the ROI
from the project?

00:23:09.885 --> 00:23:11.840
GEORGE: Yeah, exactly.

00:23:13.020 --> 00:23:14.510
If it's going to take you,

00:23:15.210 --> 00:23:16.710
let's say, eight months

00:23:17.370 --> 00:23:22.935
to make this tenth of a percent
increase, is that worth it? I mean,

00:23:24.190 --> 00:23:27.660
and then other factors, well,
maybe in a couple of years, we

00:23:27.660 --> 00:23:29.170
know things will change.

00:23:29.170 --> 00:23:32.460
So maybe that tenth of a
percent isn't even going to

00:23:32.460 --> 00:23:35.460
last us that long because
things will change,

00:23:35.460 --> 00:23:36.860
and then alter that, so.

00:23:36.860 --> 00:23:37.520
- Yeah,

00:23:37.520 --> 00:23:39.990
I didn't use tenth of a percent
because it's like a good

00:23:39.990 --> 00:23:42.435
target. I used tenth
of a percent just

00:23:43.950 --> 00:23:45.825
because I was writing
this five minutes ago.

00:23:47.160 --> 00:23:47.660
But

00:23:48.630 --> 00:23:50.310
you should have, obviously--

00:23:50.310 --> 00:23:52.590
I think a lot of times
the benefit needs to just be

00:23:52.590 --> 00:23:55.530
unquestionably worth
the time investment.

00:23:57.160 --> 00:23:58.090
GEORGE: Yeah.

00:23:58.090 --> 00:23:58.890
- It

00:24:00.250 --> 00:24:02.830
often just doesn't come up
because it's just so obviously--

00:24:02.830 --> 00:24:03.330
the

00:24:06.220 --> 00:24:08.950
reward to investment ratio is
should be high enough that it's

00:24:08.950 --> 00:24:12.040
just not a hang up. If
it is getting that marginally close,

00:24:12.040 --> 00:24:15.820
it's probably not something to
pursue. Because let's face it,

00:24:15.820 --> 00:24:17.485
most ideas fall flat.

00:24:18.590 --> 00:24:21.300
GEORGE: Yeah, I guess
I'm kind of really

00:24:22.180 --> 00:24:23.750
interested in this
question because

00:24:24.640 --> 00:24:29.440
we have a small data science team,
and we get a lot of different--

00:24:29.440 --> 00:24:33.460
we have a lot of needs. We
have a lot of requests. And we

00:24:35.440 --> 00:24:39.635
support the lab
at GPL or we got a

00:24:41.120 --> 00:24:46.235
lot of different needs, but we
also just have very focused areas.

00:24:46.940 --> 00:24:49.790
So we kind of have
to run things through like, well,

00:24:49.790 --> 00:24:53.930
does this support the vision?
Our vision statement here,

00:24:53.930 --> 00:24:56.440
does it support the
strategy but also--

00:24:57.140 --> 00:25:00.590
yeah, we have 10 projects
that are great ideas,

00:25:00.590 --> 00:25:01.760
but which of those 10

00:25:03.290 --> 00:25:06.620
would be the best ROI?
Would have the best ROI?

00:25:06.620 --> 00:25:10.885
So it's all about I
think for me competing priorities, so

00:25:11.600 --> 00:25:13.970
with a limited amount
of resources we have.

00:25:17.045 --> 00:25:18.800
- I have found it
really helpful,

00:25:18.800 --> 00:25:20.944
like as I've gotten more
into Bayesian statistics

00:25:22.870 --> 00:25:26.395
this idea of a
prior probability, particularly around success.

00:25:29.660 --> 00:25:30.470
And this

00:25:31.430 --> 00:25:33.230
to me maps exactly
with your question.

00:25:33.230 --> 00:25:34.350
It's like you have multiple

00:25:35.210 --> 00:25:39.830
potential projects you could
pursue. And it's like,

00:25:41.360 --> 00:25:44.060
you and your stakeholders are
like really the experts in this

00:25:44.060 --> 00:25:45.080
space at the company.

00:25:45.860 --> 00:25:48.440
Put a prior probability
of success around it.

00:25:48.440 --> 00:25:51.695
What is the range of
outcomes that, going into the project,

00:25:52.730 --> 00:25:56.420
it might realize? It might
manifest, that is. And then,

00:25:57.380 --> 00:25:59.840
I think that could be
enormously helpful in helping

00:25:59.840 --> 00:26:02.390
you decide, is this something
we should explore more,

00:26:02.390 --> 00:26:04.250
or is this something that's
like promising enough that like

00:26:04.250 --> 00:26:05.900
it's time to go do it?

00:26:07.340 --> 00:26:10.610
But again, I think prior to

00:26:12.630 --> 00:26:13.930
my getting

00:26:14.790 --> 00:26:15.840
into Bayesian statistics,

00:26:15.840 --> 00:26:18.670
that idea would not have been
as clear to me that like, oh,

00:26:18.670 --> 00:26:21.870
I could actually go into a
project. And I always do, in fact,

00:26:21.870 --> 00:26:25.380
go into projects with a
prior estimate of how successful it's

00:26:25.380 --> 00:26:28.950
likely to be what the range of
effect sizes I might reasonably expect are.

00:26:28.950 --> 00:26:31.680
I just wasn't making
it explicit before I learned

00:26:31.680 --> 00:26:33.000
Bayesian statistics.

00:26:34.380 --> 00:26:36.080
But it's hugely important. If

00:26:37.020 --> 00:26:39.030
everything you do has a
low prior probability of success,

00:26:39.030 --> 00:26:41.340
you're just generating noise, so.

00:26:42.090 --> 00:26:45.240
GEORGE: That is helpful.
I appreciate that. Thanks.

00:26:45.240 --> 00:26:46.390
- I'd love to--

00:26:48.160 --> 00:26:49.780
someone just about
to ask a question.

00:26:49.780 --> 00:26:52.105
WOMAN: I was, I can't figure
out how to raise my hand today.

00:26:52.105 --> 00:26:53.020
RACHAEL: Go ahead.

00:26:53.020 --> 00:26:54.640
WOMAN: I'm so sorry about that.

00:26:57.100 --> 00:26:59.870
Have you guys ever run
into an issue where

00:27:00.550 --> 00:27:04.390
you have to make a change
because maybe it's compliance

00:27:04.390 --> 00:27:07.630
or legal and you still have
to then estimate like the amount

00:27:07.630 --> 00:27:09.800
of time it's going to
take to do that, where the

00:27:10.480 --> 00:27:13.210
return on investment is
not necessarily like--

00:27:13.210 --> 00:27:15.820
there may be dollars
saved to do this,

00:27:15.820 --> 00:27:18.770
but you also
are legally required to

00:27:19.570 --> 00:27:20.650
make those changes.

00:27:20.650 --> 00:27:21.100
And so then,

00:27:21.100 --> 00:27:24.790
you have to plan out your
project from that perspective.

00:27:26.150 --> 00:27:29.390
I know working with hospitals,
there's a lot of compliance

00:27:29.390 --> 00:27:31.910
regulations and so curious
how you guys approach those

00:27:33.320 --> 00:27:34.070
kinds of requests.

00:27:34.745 --> 00:27:35.000
-

00:27:35.000 --> 00:27:37.370
If we have to make a
change within one of our own projects

00:27:37.370 --> 00:27:39.470
that we make the change.

00:27:39.470 --> 00:27:43.490
There's no discussion really about how
long is this going to take?

00:27:43.490 --> 00:27:46.850
Or what is the return here
because we've already got the

00:27:46.850 --> 00:27:51.110
return or know what the return
is or has been from that

00:27:51.110 --> 00:27:53.030
project or that initiative.

00:27:53.810 --> 00:27:56.300
So in the case of
something where it's like compliance or

00:27:56.300 --> 00:27:59.810
security or critical upgrade,
we're just going to do it.

00:27:59.810 --> 00:28:01.670
There's no question about it
we're just going to do it.

00:28:02.550 --> 00:28:04.835
WOMAN: Yeah, I've
run into issues where

00:28:05.960 --> 00:28:08.170
we're trying to do something
because it's a potential

00:28:08.870 --> 00:28:10.590
compliance thing.

00:28:10.590 --> 00:28:12.650
And so you get into that hairy
thing where it's like, OK,

00:28:12.650 --> 00:28:15.080
well, maybe it's fine,
but maybe it's not fine.

00:28:15.080 --> 00:28:19.160
And if they decide that it's
not fine, then it's a problem.

00:28:19.160 --> 00:28:23.300
But that like living in that
fuzzy area where we'd like to

00:28:23.300 --> 00:28:27.465
invest something earlier than
before it's a huge problem

00:28:28.260 --> 00:28:31.870
becomes a weird thing
because saying like, well,

00:28:31.870 --> 00:28:35.010
this could be an all or
nothing situation if we are

00:28:35.010 --> 00:28:38.400
non-compliant is not really something
that I've had a lot

00:28:38.400 --> 00:28:42.945
of success bringing up to
prioritizing those projects.

00:28:44.730 --> 00:28:45.230
- So

00:28:45.900 --> 00:28:47.460
I want to make sure
I understand your question. So, obviously,

00:28:47.460 --> 00:28:49.440
anything that's like a question
of legal compliance is just

00:28:49.440 --> 00:28:51.540
like, yeah, I got do it.

00:28:52.320 --> 00:28:54.270
WOMAN: Well, sometimes, we

00:28:55.230 --> 00:28:57.840
get documentation from
government partners or

00:28:57.840 --> 00:28:59.280
regulatory agencies.
That's like, hey,

00:28:59.280 --> 00:29:00.900
these are proposed changes.

00:29:00.900 --> 00:29:03.270
We don't know if we're
going to lock these in yet,

00:29:03.270 --> 00:29:04.890
but it could happen.

00:29:05.430 --> 00:29:10.290
We could decide when we release the
final version of this to do this.

00:29:10.290 --> 00:29:13.560
And so you have a six-month
window where you've got to size

00:29:14.910 --> 00:29:15.997
up what

00:29:17.190 --> 00:29:18.360
that difference is.

00:29:18.360 --> 00:29:21.000
- OK, so I understand
this better now.

00:29:21.720 --> 00:29:23.880
And I'm sorry if my
answer sounded pedantic before. I

00:29:25.260 --> 00:29:27.300
was hoping it would lead
into something more. But

00:29:28.700 --> 00:29:32.060
so if there's this potential thing
that could happen is what you're saying.

00:29:32.060 --> 00:29:32.600
WOMAN: Yeah.

00:29:32.600 --> 00:29:34.880
JOE: That could impact
you, OK. So, again,

00:29:34.880 --> 00:29:38.060
I find this Bayesian idea
of an expected loss really helpful.

00:29:39.170 --> 00:29:41.060
OK, and this is when--

00:29:41.060 --> 00:29:42.200
yeah, you don't know.

00:29:42.710 --> 00:29:44.750
The future is uncertain. You
don't know what's going to

00:29:44.750 --> 00:29:47.720
happen. But there's some
probability it's going to happen,

00:29:47.720 --> 00:29:51.800
and there's a range of
penalties or losses you could

00:29:51.800 --> 00:29:53.120
incur if it does happen.

00:29:54.320 --> 00:29:56.930
And a real simple example is
that if there's a 10% chance

00:29:56.930 --> 00:29:58.070
you're going to lose a dollar,

00:29:58.070 --> 00:30:02.525
you're expected loss is 10%
of a dollar or 10 pennies,

00:30:03.350 --> 00:30:05.510
versus a 90% chance you're
going to lose a dollar.

00:30:05.510 --> 00:30:08.480
That's a 90 cent
expected loss. And so

00:30:09.350 --> 00:30:11.930
I think given that uncertainty,

00:30:11.930 --> 00:30:13.520
it's not a question of
like breaking the law,

00:30:13.520 --> 00:30:15.420
but your question
is around like, oh,

00:30:15.420 --> 00:30:16.790
we don't know if there's going
to be these regulations in the

00:30:16.790 --> 00:30:18.950
future. How much
might this impact us?

00:30:18.950 --> 00:30:21.365
I think this expected
loss framework can really help

00:30:24.430 --> 00:30:27.670
support rational decision
making in an uncertain future,

00:30:27.670 --> 00:30:30.370
rather than always jumping to
the most extreme worst case

00:30:30.370 --> 00:30:35.050
scenario, which is not an
optimal way to make decisions.

00:30:37.210 --> 00:30:37.710
Yeah.

00:30:39.132 --> 00:30:41.940
RACHAEL: I want to echo
what somebody said in the chat,

00:30:41.940 --> 00:30:44.790
and it's a phone number, so
I don't know who it is. But

00:30:46.240 --> 00:30:49.140
it does seem like we're
discussing different, and they

00:30:49.140 --> 00:30:52.260
said, and equally important
methods of establishing ROI.

00:30:52.260 --> 00:30:55.140
And I'm realizing that as
well that I think, in my head,

00:30:55.140 --> 00:30:59.070
I always thought of like ROI as
like you have to come up with

00:30:59.070 --> 00:31:02.450
this accounting number and the actual

00:31:04.350 --> 00:31:08.060
number that it's going to
impact in terms of revenue versus

00:31:09.000 --> 00:31:11.144
thinking of the statistical

00:31:12.120 --> 00:31:14.790
side of things as well. So
it's really interesting.

00:31:14.790 --> 00:31:17.010
- There was a good question
in the chat, Rachael,

00:31:17.010 --> 00:31:21.930
about how to handle when
your project underperforms relative to projections.

00:31:21.930 --> 00:31:23.280
- Yeah, I was going to have JT

00:31:24.090 --> 00:31:27.075
asked that one live. JT, if
you want to jump in here.

00:31:28.716 --> 00:31:31.850
- Yeah, sorry if my
treadmill was a little loud. I

00:31:31.850 --> 00:31:33.470
don't have my headphones
in right now.

00:31:33.470 --> 00:31:35.210
DEREK: No, we can't hear
it. We can't hear it.

00:31:35.210 --> 00:31:35.820
JT: Cool.

00:31:35.820 --> 00:31:38.970
- And shout out to the cats
who code t-shirt, yeah.

00:31:38.970 --> 00:31:39.806
[LAUGHTER]

00:31:41.060 --> 00:31:41.930
- Yeah, I

00:31:41.930 --> 00:31:45.860
guess I was just wondering how
you handle if a project

00:31:45.860 --> 00:31:48.485
that you've had to pitch
and sort of fight for,

00:31:49.220 --> 00:31:52.100
how you handle if maybe
it under-delivers or

00:31:52.100 --> 00:31:53.540
underperforms, as you said.

00:31:54.470 --> 00:31:58.655
Mostly, I guess I'm wondering
about the trust aspect of

00:31:59.600 --> 00:32:02.540
like the decision
makers or stakeholders,

00:32:03.170 --> 00:32:08.210
how that impacts any future
proposals you have for projects.

00:32:08.210 --> 00:32:09.040
- So

00:32:09.740 --> 00:32:13.460
you got to remember, A, there's
variability in the world.

00:32:13.460 --> 00:32:15.560
And B, you can't
control everything.

00:32:15.560 --> 00:32:18.410
And the thing you can control
though is transparency.

00:32:19.430 --> 00:32:22.160
And so I think that's
just really key that, one,

00:32:24.170 --> 00:32:25.250
your findings--

00:32:25.250 --> 00:32:28.160
so your ROI projections
should reflect a range

00:32:29.300 --> 00:32:30.155
to begin with.

00:32:30.980 --> 00:32:31.560
I mean,

00:32:31.560 --> 00:32:35.360
even just mean and interval is
like a great anchor point of

00:32:35.360 --> 00:32:38.090
just to remind them. We
don't know the future with certainty.

00:32:38.090 --> 00:32:41.360
This is the reasonable range
of outcomes we might expect.

00:32:43.490 --> 00:32:48.080
And then just to be transparent
throughout about how you--

00:32:48.080 --> 00:32:49.250
without drowning
them in detail.

00:32:49.250 --> 00:32:51.440
But just be transparent
about how you arrived at your

00:32:51.440 --> 00:32:52.643
numbers, and then what

00:32:53.370 --> 00:32:54.890
you saw at end of
season. I mean,

00:32:54.890 --> 00:32:57.290
there have certainly
been projects, even successful

00:32:57.290 --> 00:33:00.695
projects, that have been on
that along the way fell short

00:33:01.610 --> 00:33:02.892
because we

00:33:04.220 --> 00:33:05.915
were unaware of
some key factor.

00:33:08.550 --> 00:33:11.580
And then you learn because
it doesn't deliver the way that

00:33:11.580 --> 00:33:12.900
you expected it to.

00:33:12.900 --> 00:33:13.400
And

00:33:14.580 --> 00:33:17.340
never during that did I feel
like the leaders who had

00:33:17.340 --> 00:33:21.870
invested in us were upset
or frustrated. It was like,

00:33:21.870 --> 00:33:24.510
look, we leaned into
a difficult problem,

00:33:24.510 --> 00:33:27.885
and we learned along the way.
And just be clear about it.

00:33:27.885 --> 00:33:29.160
This was what we overlooked.

00:33:29.160 --> 00:33:32.220
This is the correction we put in
place as we pursue it next time.

00:33:33.570 --> 00:33:34.380
And I think that

00:33:35.460 --> 00:33:37.230
that transparency
and honesty is

00:33:37.890 --> 00:33:38.740
the most you can do.

00:33:38.740 --> 00:33:40.080
But again, just also
anchor them to a--

00:33:40.080 --> 00:33:42.870
just get them thinking ahead of
time about there's a range of

00:33:42.870 --> 00:33:47.040
outcomes we could experience even
if we did introduce this

00:33:47.040 --> 00:33:50.310
through improvement
of 1% or 2%.

00:33:51.150 --> 00:33:53.070
It's sampling variation. It

00:33:53.670 --> 00:33:57.390
could show up in our customer sample
for the year as a lot of

00:33:57.390 --> 00:34:01.080
different values. That's
not how normal people think.

00:34:01.080 --> 00:34:04.570
Normal people think
very causally, narratively, precisely.

00:34:05.430 --> 00:34:07.823
They don't think in terms of
variance. So you have to condition

00:34:08.590 --> 00:34:09.090
your

00:34:09.940 --> 00:34:12.040
key stakeholders to

00:34:12.670 --> 00:34:14.260
expect that kind of variance.

00:34:14.260 --> 00:34:16.900
And they'll appreciate
it in my experience.

00:34:18.391 --> 00:34:22.760
- I wanted to remind everybody,
if you do want to provide some

00:34:22.760 --> 00:34:25.790
perspective here, too, and
