00:34:25.790 --> 00:34:29.219
feel free to raise your hand just
so I know to call on you.

00:34:29.219 --> 00:34:32.540
It's in the Zoom bar below
if you click on reactions.

00:34:32.540 --> 00:34:34.849
You'll have a raise
hand. Sorry, Derek.

00:34:34.849 --> 00:34:36.290
- No worries. That's great.

00:34:37.070 --> 00:34:38.449
So to the under-deliver part,

00:34:39.380 --> 00:34:42.830
this is probably going to be
very different to what Joe said, too.

00:34:42.830 --> 00:34:47.630
But we have maybe like four
different ways in which we can

00:34:47.630 --> 00:34:49.410
expect and/or watch

00:34:51.050 --> 00:34:53.135
potential or actual
under-delivered

00:34:53.929 --> 00:34:57.065
projects. So early in our
baseline, trying to establish

00:34:58.280 --> 00:35:00.350
what the effects
really might be.

00:35:00.350 --> 00:35:04.910
If it looks like it's not
quite as big of say an issue

00:35:04.910 --> 00:35:06.040
or feasible

00:35:06.860 --> 00:35:10.190
for us to pursue it, and
we suspect it would be

00:35:10.910 --> 00:35:13.460
something that under-delivers
on the potential impact,

00:35:13.460 --> 00:35:14.390
we're not going to do it.

00:35:14.990 --> 00:35:18.450
During model building, if we
do not cross certain acceptable

00:35:18.450 --> 00:35:22.500
thresholds in particular things around like
precision recall or PBV and NPV,

00:35:23.160 --> 00:35:25.650
we have some deployments
like do not miss.

00:35:25.650 --> 00:35:27.960
You just absolutely do
not miss a prediction.

00:35:29.640 --> 00:35:31.680
If the models aren't
performing well enough,

00:35:31.680 --> 00:35:33.670
we will not go forward with it,

00:35:33.670 --> 00:35:36.975
which seems probably pretty typical.
But then, we get into

00:35:38.160 --> 00:35:39.555
a little bit later where

00:35:40.440 --> 00:35:44.190
say in kind of like an
earlier silent deployment,

00:35:44.190 --> 00:35:45.525
we can watch
things for a while,

00:35:46.320 --> 00:35:49.350
be very cautious and safe
for some of our stuff.

00:35:51.420 --> 00:35:54.390
And think Chloe's here so she
can definitely jump in and talk

00:35:54.390 --> 00:35:55.310
a lot about this

00:35:56.220 --> 00:35:57.300
for some of the work we do.

00:35:59.230 --> 00:36:01.870
And sorry if this kind of
gets a little maybe ML-opsy,

00:36:01.870 --> 00:36:04.810
but we have two types
of monitoring we do.

00:36:05.530 --> 00:36:09.710
One is we monitor models,
and we monitor the intervention.

00:36:09.710 --> 00:36:14.080
So the model may target one
thing while its use is meant to

00:36:14.080 --> 00:36:15.190
impact something else.

00:36:15.190 --> 00:36:17.695
And those are the primary
metrics that I showed.

00:36:18.700 --> 00:36:24.070
If we're not seeing, say,
an actual impact or something is

00:36:24.070 --> 00:36:28.855
going in the wrong direction.
That for us is under-deliver, and

00:36:29.590 --> 00:36:30.595
you just stop.

00:36:32.020 --> 00:36:35.260
So we are also carefully
watching the intended

00:36:35.260 --> 00:36:37.970
intervention metrics here,

00:36:37.970 --> 00:36:40.660
which is the primary metrics of what
you're going to change in the hospital.

00:36:40.660 --> 00:36:42.460
And fortunately,

00:36:42.460 --> 00:36:45.370
we don't really have
anything that under-delivers because we

00:36:45.370 --> 00:36:48.160
do, like Joe
said, we understand variability.

00:36:48.160 --> 00:36:49.000
Things are very weird,

00:36:49.000 --> 00:36:52.900
especially just a smidgen
outside or still within the

00:36:52.900 --> 00:36:55.765
pandemic in health care.
Things are all over the place.

00:36:57.070 --> 00:36:57.570
But

00:36:58.090 --> 00:37:01.010
we've been talking a lot about
this in the context of one of

00:37:01.010 --> 00:37:02.030
our particular projects where

00:37:03.520 --> 00:37:04.270
in the--

00:37:04.270 --> 00:37:08.290
or in the post phase, it's
been deployed. It's being used,

00:37:09.070 --> 00:37:11.860
made a big impact,
dropped readmission rates.

00:37:11.860 --> 00:37:15.040
And we're seeing over time,
it's drifting back up.

00:37:15.040 --> 00:37:16.130
So we have this really,

00:37:16.130 --> 00:37:17.380
really uncomfortable
question right now.

00:37:17.380 --> 00:37:19.540
What if we pull it, does
everything just jump back up,

00:37:19.540 --> 00:37:22.030
or is this a natural trend
back up where something about the

00:37:22.030 --> 00:37:23.635
deployment a year
later is not quite

00:37:24.790 --> 00:37:25.480
actually working?

00:37:27.570 --> 00:37:29.190
So, yeah, we have to
start thinking about this.

00:37:29.190 --> 00:37:30.750
We didn't really under-deliver,

00:37:30.750 --> 00:37:33.390
but we're starting
to lose some ground.

00:37:33.390 --> 00:37:35.115
And we don't know
if it's actually

00:37:35.850 --> 00:37:37.800
what we did or if
it's something bigger,

00:37:37.800 --> 00:37:40.800
which is probably the case
within an exhausted health care

00:37:40.800 --> 00:37:44.100
system, where readmission rates are
just kind of up in general.

00:37:45.540 --> 00:37:48.450
So this is actually a super
tricky for us because we're at

00:37:48.450 --> 00:37:50.040
a point where we
need to figure out.

00:37:51.430 --> 00:37:55.075
As the direction of an intervention
is kind of losing some ground,

00:37:55.750 --> 00:37:56.620
what do we do?

00:37:58.036 --> 00:38:00.700
- I'm thinking also, as
we talk about this that--

00:38:00.700 --> 00:38:01.770
we mentioned earlier,

00:38:01.770 --> 00:38:04.860
those metrics that your stakeholders
all align on and value,

00:38:07.050 --> 00:38:08.040
I'm thinking also about just

00:38:08.880 --> 00:38:14.220
explicitly redefining those metrics in
some cases is often really valuable.

00:38:14.220 --> 00:38:17.925
And this goes to
that underperformance issue is that

00:38:18.720 --> 00:38:21.540
two people can mean completely
different things by the same metric.

00:38:21.540 --> 00:38:22.040
And

00:38:23.160 --> 00:38:25.380
if you both
explicitly defined it,

00:38:25.380 --> 00:38:26.890
and then thought
ahead about what

00:38:27.570 --> 00:38:28.740
are potential ways where--

00:38:29.370 --> 00:38:31.950
I like Derek's
monitoring question--

00:38:31.950 --> 00:38:34.090
or monitoring answer a
lot. Like what are things--

00:38:34.090 --> 00:38:35.340
what are ways
things can go awry,

00:38:35.340 --> 00:38:36.600
and how can you
get ahead of that?

00:38:38.250 --> 00:38:41.640
So like you think about
A/B testing or experimentation and

00:38:41.640 --> 00:38:42.750
science. Like,

00:38:44.070 --> 00:38:46.170
science really cares
about false positives.

00:38:46.170 --> 00:38:50.580
They don't want false positive
effects getting into that body of

00:38:51.420 --> 00:38:52.710
scientific knowledge.

00:38:53.510 --> 00:38:56.270
I think that's a lot less
important in industry.

00:38:56.270 --> 00:38:57.950
It matters in medicine,
so I'm going to--

00:38:57.950 --> 00:39:00.890
I'll say to Derek case,
this is not his approach.

00:39:00.890 --> 00:39:04.250
But if you think about
in tech, a lot of times,

00:39:04.250 --> 00:39:05.855
a false positive
is a free mistake.

00:39:06.680 --> 00:39:11.090
All the development costs are sunk
by the time that the test runs.

00:39:11.090 --> 00:39:15.560
If you release some minor
change that turns out to be

00:39:15.560 --> 00:39:16.790
like a false positive result,

00:39:18.020 --> 00:39:19.550
no one's harmed by this.

00:39:19.550 --> 00:39:21.680
It's actually the false
negatives in tech that are

00:39:21.680 --> 00:39:22.490
really dangerous.

00:39:22.490 --> 00:39:24.800
And those are not
the priority in statistical significance

00:39:24.800 --> 00:39:26.690
testing in other areas.

00:39:26.690 --> 00:39:28.490
The reason I'm bringing
all this up is like,

00:39:28.490 --> 00:39:31.340
so I work on experimentation.
Things that I get--

00:39:31.340 --> 00:39:33.710
metrics that matter to me
and my stakeholders are like

00:39:33.710 --> 00:39:35.200
accuracy and

00:39:36.080 --> 00:39:37.770
duration or speed.

00:39:37.770 --> 00:39:39.650
So like, well, what's accuracy?

00:39:39.650 --> 00:39:42.130
Is accuracy avoiding false
positives? They're free mistakes?

00:39:42.830 --> 00:39:47.180
Or can I redefine accuracy
as like some kind of optimization

00:39:47.180 --> 00:39:49.850
rate that's focused more
on detecting true effects than

00:39:49.850 --> 00:39:50.855
avoiding false effects?

00:39:51.890 --> 00:39:52.870
That kind of shift,

00:39:54.000 --> 00:39:56.010
accuracy is like a term we
can all throw around. But

00:39:56.610 --> 00:40:00.450
you could completely redefine
that and justifiably.

00:40:00.450 --> 00:40:03.270
But you need to get your
stakeholders on board that, hey, look,

00:40:03.270 --> 00:40:06.720
we're not going to key
your index on false positive rates.

00:40:06.720 --> 00:40:08.850
That's not what matters
to us as a business.

00:40:09.780 --> 00:40:10.350
And again,

00:40:11.040 --> 00:40:14.160
if you've explicitly addressed that
up front, and you say, look,

00:40:14.160 --> 00:40:16.320
I think we could have better
business performance by

00:40:16.320 --> 00:40:20.280
shifting our focus toward
true positives rather than avoiding

00:40:20.280 --> 00:40:21.750
these false positives.
And then again,

00:40:21.750 --> 00:40:23.895
you do all your simulations
to show why you've got

00:40:24.510 --> 00:40:26.055
a promising way to pursue this.

00:40:26.730 --> 00:40:28.470
But you just admit up front.

00:40:28.470 --> 00:40:30.300
We're going to have
more false positives.

00:40:30.300 --> 00:40:33.210
And we're OK with that because
those are free mistakes in our

00:40:33.210 --> 00:40:34.980
area. Those are not free
mistakes in medicine.

00:40:34.980 --> 00:40:36.720
Those are not free mistakes
in a lot of other areas.

00:40:36.720 --> 00:40:38.970
But in many areas
of tech, they are.

00:40:38.970 --> 00:40:39.470
So,

00:40:40.620 --> 00:40:40.890
yeah,

00:40:40.890 --> 00:40:43.470
just kind of thinking in
advance about what are ways

00:40:43.470 --> 00:40:45.690
that things could go awry
or get misinterpreted.

00:40:45.690 --> 00:40:49.780
And then making sure that you
have the tracking system in place,

00:40:49.780 --> 00:40:51.660
which is also just all going
to come key at the end of the

00:40:51.660 --> 00:40:52.980
project when you have to show

00:40:54.730 --> 00:40:57.115
how did things you
know materialize in real life.

00:40:58.146 --> 00:41:00.800
- Yeah, definitely. And Dan,

00:41:00.800 --> 00:41:02.910
I see you have something
to add to this topic, too.

00:41:02.910 --> 00:41:03.710
You want to jump in?

00:41:03.710 --> 00:41:06.800
- Yeah, although it might
be changing the subject a little

00:41:06.800 --> 00:41:08.060
bit. So I don't want to

00:41:09.090 --> 00:41:10.380
change if that's not

00:41:10.980 --> 00:41:12.360
what you want to do.

00:41:13.040 --> 00:41:14.460
- Oh, that's OK.

00:41:14.460 --> 00:41:17.280
But does anybody else feel
like this conversation could go on

00:41:17.280 --> 00:41:18.390
for three hours about?

00:41:19.340 --> 00:41:21.540
I feel like there's so
much to talk about here.

00:41:22.472 --> 00:41:23.660
- Yeah, no,

00:41:24.590 --> 00:41:26.240
sorry, I'm battling
a cold, too.

00:41:27.020 --> 00:41:29.660
I feel like I'm the
only non-data scientist on the phone.

00:41:29.660 --> 00:41:33.650
So the conversation about Bayesian
statistics is a little bit--

00:41:33.650 --> 00:41:36.410
oh, good. Thanks, Rachael,
make me feel good here.

00:41:37.690 --> 00:41:39.460
The things that I'm thinking
about are maybe a little bit

00:41:39.460 --> 00:41:40.190
more fundamental.

00:41:40.190 --> 00:41:43.507
I think what Derek and Joe
are talking about are really

00:41:44.760 --> 00:41:47.400
high level. I mean, this
is like professional.

00:41:47.400 --> 00:41:49.545
I know, Joe, you said
you'd never been taught this, but

00:41:50.190 --> 00:41:52.050
I feel like you could
teach the class on it.

00:41:53.820 --> 00:41:55.170
The things that I think about--

00:41:55.170 --> 00:41:56.670
- Had to learn something
in just 10 years.

00:41:56.670 --> 00:41:58.005
DAN: Yeah, exactly.

00:41:59.430 --> 00:42:02.295
What I think about this
is, what does the company need?

00:42:03.780 --> 00:42:04.740
And think Derek's

00:42:05.460 --> 00:42:09.600
example that he showed was just
so perfect of this. Because

00:42:10.410 --> 00:42:12.810
like, it didn't do it on
purpose, or it did it on

00:42:12.810 --> 00:42:15.450
purpose, but it wasn't like in
your face. But it was like,

00:42:15.450 --> 00:42:17.475
these are the metrics the
company actually cares about.

00:42:18.730 --> 00:42:19.945
How is what you're doing

00:42:20.710 --> 00:42:22.060
fixing those metrics?

00:42:22.850 --> 00:42:24.250
So I think what we get a lot,

00:42:24.250 --> 00:42:28.360
what I tend to see is
passionate data scientists. And

00:42:28.360 --> 00:42:31.000
they're going off, and they
see stuff, and they're like, oh,

00:42:31.000 --> 00:42:32.500
I bet I could do this.

00:42:33.540 --> 00:42:37.200
But it doesn't come from this
direct line from strategy.

00:42:37.200 --> 00:42:38.445
So I love Derek's

00:42:39.450 --> 00:42:42.670
presentation of it because it's like,
tell me where it is here.

00:42:42.670 --> 00:42:43.470
So I think that is--

00:42:43.470 --> 00:42:45.480
if we had to take one
thing away from it,

00:42:45.480 --> 00:42:47.280
I would love to take that away.

00:42:47.280 --> 00:42:49.350
- Yeah, no, 100% agree.

00:42:49.350 --> 00:42:52.455
Put it in the metrics
that your non-technical stakeholders care about.

00:42:53.340 --> 00:42:56.010
Yeah, which is probably
not area under the curve.

00:42:56.010 --> 00:42:57.780
It's like live saved,

00:42:59.490 --> 00:43:02.880
empty beds, et cetera. Yeah,
just put it in those terms.

00:43:02.880 --> 00:43:04.800
And the data scientists
will be compelled, too.

00:43:04.800 --> 00:43:08.100
so let's just go for
that lowest common denominator for persuasion.

00:43:08.600 --> 00:43:11.890
- And then my next point
that I'll get off my soapbox here.

00:43:11.890 --> 00:43:15.265
But my next point was, why
are they asking about ROI?

00:43:16.440 --> 00:43:17.898
And sometimes,

00:43:19.140 --> 00:43:23.475
they ask about ROI because
it's a multi-billion dollar decision,

00:43:24.240 --> 00:43:26.220
and so the CFO needs
to know. And so

00:43:26.880 --> 00:43:29.220
100%, you need to
go dig into this.

00:43:29.220 --> 00:43:31.095
But I think sometimes
they ask about ROI

00:43:31.950 --> 00:43:33.165
because they don't
think it's valuable.

00:43:33.900 --> 00:43:36.960
And they're just asking you,
prove your value on this.

00:43:36.960 --> 00:43:38.670
And then, I think, sometimes, there

00:43:38.670 --> 00:43:41.250
are projects that are devoid
of any kind of corporate

00:43:41.250 --> 00:43:43.110
metrics or anything like
that. And so they're saying,

00:43:43.110 --> 00:43:46.080
what's the value? So you can
do all the work you want,

00:43:46.080 --> 00:43:47.640
you're never going
to convince them.

00:43:48.540 --> 00:43:53.625
So you got to pull it back
to strategy somehow and bring that forward.

00:43:54.862 --> 00:43:56.480
- I guess don't see those as--

00:43:56.480 --> 00:43:58.940
I don't see those as competing.
Like, if you have a strategy,

00:43:58.940 --> 00:44:03.110
a good strategy should be
anchored to a clear goal.

00:44:03.110 --> 00:44:04.700
And then the company,
your leaders,

00:44:04.700 --> 00:44:09.140
are aligning resources to ensure
the successful pursuit of that goal.

00:44:09.890 --> 00:44:11.210
I guess, I

00:44:11.930 --> 00:44:13.766
don't see the conflict at all.

00:44:13.766 --> 00:44:17.015
DAN: No, that's right. The
conflict is with your--

00:44:19.120 --> 00:44:23.290
it sounded like your
organizations are very structured.

00:44:23.290 --> 00:44:26.350
And I think maybe some of
the sciencey organizations,

00:44:26.350 --> 00:44:27.205
it's a little bit looser,

00:44:27.940 --> 00:44:29.575
and so you get a
little bit more

00:44:30.670 --> 00:44:35.020
exploration. And that that's
not very easy to tie back

00:44:35.020 --> 00:44:37.120
directly to strategy by--

00:44:37.120 --> 00:44:39.280
- This is a good point.
This is a good point.

00:44:39.280 --> 00:44:41.380
Some things are like--

00:44:41.380 --> 00:44:42.760
I can think around like,

00:44:44.440 --> 00:44:48.400
skill development among the
data scientists and analysts.

00:44:49.060 --> 00:44:50.590
It's something where it's,
intuitively, it's like, yeah,

00:44:50.590 --> 00:44:53.560
if we invest in this,
this should pay dividends.

00:44:53.560 --> 00:44:57.070
That will be very,
very hard to measure.

00:44:57.950 --> 00:44:58.685
But it's like--

00:45:00.740 --> 00:45:02.720
or the best measurement you're
going to get is probably

00:45:02.720 --> 00:45:04.625
anecdotes and case studies. But

00:45:06.020 --> 00:45:07.670
is it still worth doing?

00:45:07.670 --> 00:45:09.080
Yes, but again,

00:45:09.080 --> 00:45:10.970
you're going to have to be
transparent up front. That it's like,

00:45:10.970 --> 00:45:13.130
we're not going to be able
to measure this with x

00:45:13.970 --> 00:45:16.700
percent increase conversions
or decrease mortalities.

00:45:16.700 --> 00:45:20.455
But it's like, where do
the moonshot ideas come from? Again,

00:45:22.580 --> 00:45:23.390
it kind of depends on--

00:45:23.390 --> 00:45:26.300
you have to know your
leadership and what do they

00:45:26.300 --> 00:45:27.470
have appetite for?

00:45:27.470 --> 00:45:27.970
If

00:45:28.790 --> 00:45:30.740
they have some like
visionary bandwidth where they're willing

00:45:30.740 --> 00:45:32.590
to float some of
those moonshot--

00:45:33.170 --> 00:45:34.460
education's a moonshot idea.

00:45:34.460 --> 00:45:37.790
But if they're willing
to entertain some of those

00:45:38.420 --> 00:45:40.910
less clear investments, good.

00:45:40.910 --> 00:45:43.940
I think you could frame it
as part of a good investment portfolio.

00:45:43.940 --> 00:45:44.710
DAN: Yeah.

00:45:44.710 --> 00:45:46.970
- But, yeah, you

00:45:48.140 --> 00:45:50.450
can't measure those
things nearly as easily.

00:45:50.450 --> 00:45:51.950
So it's harder to sell.

00:45:51.950 --> 00:45:54.425
- Thank you. My point
was just to say that

00:45:55.130 --> 00:45:57.860
the people trying to do those
moonshots might get a lot of

00:45:57.860 --> 00:46:03.155
questions about ROI, might do as much
work as they can, and still never

00:46:04.010 --> 00:46:05.060
make it. So

00:46:06.380 --> 00:46:09.020
when you get those questions,
I think it's just worth thinking,

00:46:09.020 --> 00:46:10.700
why are they asking me this?

00:46:12.110 --> 00:46:14.930
How do we position this? Is
it possible to convince them?

00:46:14.930 --> 00:46:15.530
- OK.

00:46:16.356 --> 00:46:16.770
-

00:46:16.770 --> 00:46:21.150
Something I thought might be
helpful to add to is sometimes

00:46:21.150 --> 00:46:23.685
they're not asking for the ROI.

00:46:24.360 --> 00:46:28.830
You might be sharing what you
think the potential ROI is to

00:46:28.830 --> 00:46:30.840
help push your
own idea forward,

00:46:31.770 --> 00:46:34.500
and that they're not
necessarily asking you what's

00:46:34.500 --> 00:46:37.395
the specific ROI of this.
And that's something that

00:46:38.160 --> 00:46:40.380
I've been thinking
about a lot lately.

00:46:40.380 --> 00:46:43.800
Like when I have an idea for
something, and I want to go run

00:46:43.800 --> 00:46:48.450
to share it with our
leadership team like putting together the

00:46:48.450 --> 00:46:52.110
business case for that and
explaining this is why I think

00:46:52.110 --> 00:46:53.610
this is a good idea.

00:46:53.610 --> 00:46:57.450
Here's the examples where
this has come up, or--

00:46:58.470 --> 00:47:01.980
and I know one other non-data
scientist that I saw was

00:47:01.980 --> 00:47:05.070
listening in, is our
president here at Posit,

00:47:05.070 --> 00:47:08.860
and he's often one of the people
that I go to with some of these

00:47:09.600 --> 00:47:10.440
ideas. So, Tareef,

00:47:10.440 --> 00:47:13.380
if I was wondering if you
had anything you wanted to add to

00:47:13.380 --> 00:47:14.760
the conversation to?

00:47:14.760 --> 00:47:17.200
- It's interesting. I
mean, I think both Joe

00:47:18.520 --> 00:47:20.550
and Dan raised really important

00:47:21.370 --> 00:47:25.150
points here. I like to
ask myself the question,

00:47:25.150 --> 00:47:27.730
what are the underlying
assumptions behind anything

00:47:27.730 --> 00:47:28.660
that we're doing?

00:47:29.170 --> 00:47:30.750
Whether it's on the
strategy level, what

00:47:31.450 --> 00:47:32.560
is the end goal here?

00:47:32.560 --> 00:47:35.125
And then how do I know
that I'm on the right path?

00:47:36.670 --> 00:47:37.490
That being said,

00:47:37.490 --> 00:47:40.180
you also want to leave enough
space for the moonshots.

00:47:40.180 --> 00:47:43.090
But I challenge everybody when they
say, hey, I want to do x.

00:47:43.090 --> 00:47:48.040
I'm like, why? And what are
the metrics that you are hoping to

00:47:48.040 --> 00:47:50.385
drive? It's interesting when
you show the intake form.

00:47:51.520 --> 00:47:55.000
It's intriguing because that is
an existing system that you have.

00:47:55.000 --> 00:47:57.040
The question that I find
myself asking is well, OK,

00:47:57.040 --> 00:48:01.370
how long does it take before you
know that you've got a good test, and

00:48:02.050 --> 00:48:04.360
when does the impact
sort of kick in? Because

00:48:05.140 --> 00:48:07.180
like in product development
for us when we're building new

00:48:07.180 --> 00:48:10.480
features or new products or
whatnot, it could take 18 months,

00:48:10.480 --> 00:48:12.700
24 months before you know
whether that investment was a

00:48:12.700 --> 00:48:13.780
good investment or not.

00:48:13.780 --> 00:48:14.280
But

00:48:14.950 --> 00:48:18.010
I think it's good discipline
in general to say, hey,

00:48:18.010 --> 00:48:20.455
you know what? Here's what
I'm assuming is going to change,

00:48:21.310 --> 00:48:22.870
or here's what I'm assuming
is going to get better.

00:48:22.870 --> 00:48:24.100
Here's how I'm
going to measure it.

00:48:24.880 --> 00:48:25.380
And

00:48:25.930 --> 00:48:28.420
then use that as a way
to learn and get better.

00:48:28.420 --> 00:48:30.550
That's sort of my
instinct on it.

00:48:30.550 --> 00:48:32.830
The moonshot one
is a good question.

00:48:32.830 --> 00:48:37.300
I think you might want to be
OK with saying, hey, 5% to

00:48:37.300 --> 00:48:40.030
10% of all the investments that
I make are going to be these

00:48:40.030 --> 00:48:43.795
kinds of moonshots, and I'm OK
if they fall flat on their face.

00:48:44.380 --> 00:48:47.680
JJ and I do that pretty regularly
when we say, hey, you know what?

00:48:48.640 --> 00:48:52.780
We're going to take a crack at X
or Y or Z, and we'll make an

00:48:52.780 --> 00:48:54.370
investment that we're
like, if it fails,

00:48:54.370 --> 00:48:56.680
it's not going to kill
the company. But if it succeeds,

00:48:56.680 --> 00:48:59.120
could sort of generate
a lot more revenue.

00:48:59.120 --> 00:49:00.790
So I don't know if that's
what you were looking for from me,

00:49:00.790 --> 00:49:02.860
Rachael. But I push Rachel
pretty hard when she's like,

00:49:02.860 --> 00:49:05.470
I've got seven ideas. I'm
like, great, which one matters?

00:49:08.745 --> 00:49:09.000
-

00:49:09.000 --> 00:49:11.670
There was a comment earlier
about your personal time

00:49:11.670 --> 00:49:14.310
investment, and
this echoed me was--

00:49:16.090 --> 00:49:17.680
so I used to build furniture.

00:49:17.680 --> 00:49:19.690
That was like before
I was in tech.

00:49:19.690 --> 00:49:22.720
And estimating how long a new
piece of furniture was going to

00:49:22.720 --> 00:49:23.590
take was really hard.

00:49:24.280 --> 00:49:26.560
And so when I took really good
notes, what I learned was this, that if

00:49:27.580 --> 00:49:28.975
I'd built something
similar before,

00:49:29.650 --> 00:49:31.300
I had to triple my estimate.

00:49:31.960 --> 00:49:33.820
And if I hadn't built
anything similar before,

00:49:33.820 --> 00:49:35.110
I had to multiply times nine.

00:49:36.150 --> 00:49:37.860
And friends would say like, oh,

00:49:37.860 --> 00:49:40.320
you're terrible at doing
estimates. And I'd be like, no,

00:49:40.320 --> 00:49:42.690
I'm awesome at doing estimates.

00:49:43.260 --> 00:49:44.880
And then when I
got to grad school,

00:49:44.880 --> 00:49:47.130
I found that the mechanical
engineers had names for this.

00:49:47.130 --> 00:49:48.720
They called it the rule of pi,

00:49:48.720 --> 00:49:53.340
that everything takes 3.14 times
longer than you remember it taking.

00:49:53.340 --> 00:49:56.190
And so the point of all
this was just as you think about

00:49:56.190 --> 00:49:59.220
your own time investment,
you've done all the ROI,

00:49:59.220 --> 00:50:01.380
but just making sure you
have bandwidth to see this through

00:50:01.380 --> 00:50:03.720
successfully. If you've done
something really similar

00:50:03.720 --> 00:50:05.010
before, triple it.

00:50:05.010 --> 00:50:06.960
If you've never done
something similar before,

00:50:06.960 --> 00:50:09.230
multiply times nine. And

00:50:10.050 --> 00:50:11.940
I can tell you on my
own projects, usually,

00:50:13.620 --> 00:50:16.770
that ninefold thing is just like
all the stuff you didn't think of.

00:50:16.770 --> 00:50:19.050
Like, OK, you put the
model into production, well, now,

00:50:19.050 --> 00:50:24.210
you need to train every
relevant user you need to build

00:50:24.210 --> 00:50:26.310
an enormous amount of
documentation an enormous

00:50:26.310 --> 00:50:26.970
amount of tracking.

00:50:26.970 --> 00:50:29.160
This is all the stuff that
was not part of the model

00:50:29.160 --> 00:50:30.640
development. And

00:50:31.380 --> 00:50:32.670
if you don't have
time for it, well,

00:50:32.670 --> 00:50:34.740
your life's going to be really
miserable for the rest of the

00:50:34.740 --> 00:50:37.030
year while you're working
nights and weekends to see it

00:50:37.030 --> 00:50:39.235
through, or just budget,

00:50:40.000 --> 00:50:41.800
really over budget for time,

00:50:42.580 --> 00:50:43.760
and you can have a happy life

00:50:44.500 --> 00:50:45.745
and get things going.

00:50:46.465 --> 00:50:48.910
- I just want to add one
more thing to what you just said,

00:50:48.910 --> 00:50:49.510
Joe. I love this.

00:50:49.510 --> 00:50:51.580
I'm going to think about
this pi squared idea.

00:50:52.900 --> 00:50:54.030
But the

00:50:54.910 --> 00:50:57.400
other thing that I think
is really important is to just

00:50:57.400 --> 00:51:00.250
remember that the more
things you take on simultaneously,

00:51:00.250 --> 00:51:01.840
the more your work in
progress increases,

00:51:01.840 --> 00:51:03.160
the more your
context switching.

00:51:03.160 --> 00:51:04.960
And things will
actually take longer

00:51:05.590 --> 00:51:06.940
than you even realize.

00:51:06.940 --> 00:51:07.440
So

00:51:08.470 --> 00:51:10.390
I know this is kind of like
meat and potatoes for a lot of

00:51:10.390 --> 00:51:14.470
people, but it's so easy to
get sucked in and say, oh,

00:51:14.470 --> 00:51:16.210
this is not going to take long.
I'll do this. I'll do this.

00:51:16.210 --> 00:51:19.400
Before you know it, you're
doing 17 things at the same

00:51:19.400 --> 00:51:22.700
time. And that just slows
the living daylights out of you.

00:51:23.644 --> 00:51:28.340
- This hour goes by very
quickly here. But I know if

00:51:28.340 --> 00:51:29.960
people have to run at
the top of the hour,

00:51:30.980 --> 00:51:32.600
that's OK. Thank
you for joining us.

00:51:32.600 --> 00:51:35.060
But I'm going to stay on and
hang out if people want to keep

00:51:35.060 --> 00:51:38.540
talking, too. But Brian, I saw
you had your hand raised, too,

00:51:38.540 --> 00:51:40.655
and if you want to jump in.

00:51:40.655 --> 00:51:44.370
- Yeah, so, I mean,
I come at this--

00:51:44.370 --> 00:51:45.560
so early on my career,

00:51:45.560 --> 00:51:48.770
I was actually in charge
of doing projects like this and

00:51:48.770 --> 00:51:49.910
actually presenting them

00:51:50.600 --> 00:51:54.260
as budget items for the
coast guard to Congress.

00:51:54.260 --> 00:51:55.805
And you get your ass grilled.

00:51:57.110 --> 00:51:57.905
And so,

00:51:59.310 --> 00:51:59.800
for instance,

00:51:59.800 --> 00:52:02.550
when I ran a data science
team and insurance company,

00:52:02.550 --> 00:52:04.680
all of our projects were ROI.

00:52:05.580 --> 00:52:09.540
The goal of this project is
to shave X percent off this cost

00:52:09.540 --> 00:52:11.610
it's worth X. Here
are all the variables.

00:52:11.610 --> 00:52:13.320
These are what we
think the ranges are.

00:52:13.320 --> 00:52:14.775
This is a project we should do.

00:52:16.140 --> 00:52:18.810
And the way we engage our
team to trace point, yes,

00:52:18.810 --> 00:52:20.070
when you multitask,

00:52:21.090 --> 00:52:22.440
you're really in trouble.

00:52:23.580 --> 00:52:24.900
We always had a--

00:52:24.900 --> 00:52:28.230
every team member had a
short project, the quick and easy,

00:52:28.230 --> 00:52:30.720
the medium project,
and the long project.

00:52:30.720 --> 00:52:32.340
Because in the long project,

00:52:32.340 --> 00:52:35.160
which means there was going
to be time for stakeholders to

00:52:35.160 --> 00:52:36.540
make decisions and do stuff.

00:52:36.540 --> 00:52:41.040
And they don't turn around on
a dime. So you have a midpoint,

00:52:41.040 --> 00:52:42.460
and you do a presentation.

00:52:43.170 --> 00:52:44.430
You're not going
to hear anything,

00:52:44.430 --> 00:52:45.750
feedback for two
or three weeks.

00:52:46.290 --> 00:52:48.120
That's when you can
work on other stuff.

00:52:49.650 --> 00:52:51.000
On a financial basis,

00:52:51.000 --> 00:52:54.060
I always just do stuff as
what we call real options.

00:52:54.060 --> 00:52:57.270
Your first is your exploratory
POC. What's that going to cost?

00:52:57.270 --> 00:52:59.670
What's the benefit? Benefits
probably close to zero,

00:52:59.670 --> 00:53:00.960
but it's going to be
an investment cost.

00:53:00.960 --> 00:53:05.120
But then you have an option
to kill, an option to expand,

00:53:05.880 --> 00:53:07.650
or an option to
do something else.

00:53:07.650 --> 00:53:09.810
And if you can think about
some of those options,

00:53:09.810 --> 00:53:10.960
you can price them out.

00:53:10.960 --> 00:53:13.965
And so I had to present all
my projects. Five years,

00:53:15.070 --> 00:53:15.570
here's

00:53:16.210 --> 00:53:18.475
the discounted value.
Here's the return. Because

00:53:19.555 --> 00:53:23.500
I had to go through this
CIO, who went to the CFO,

00:53:23.500 --> 00:53:24.970
and that's how they
looked at things.

00:53:24.970 --> 00:53:26.335
I want to buy

00:53:27.770 --> 00:53:28.460
ISAAC Connect,

00:53:28.460 --> 00:53:31.370
and it's cost me X amount
of bucks a year for a license.

00:53:31.370 --> 00:53:33.360
These are the benefits
I'm going to get from it.

00:53:33.360 --> 00:53:35.120
These are the amount of
people that I'm going to have.

00:53:35.120 --> 00:53:37.500
It's going to cost me X
amount of person, all these things.

00:53:37.500 --> 00:53:39.305
And so I'd have these
presentations to people,

00:53:40.900 --> 00:53:43.120
and you could go through
those discussions. And

00:53:43.990 --> 00:53:46.300
we would do variable
sensitivity,

00:53:46.300 --> 00:53:47.740
goes back to that never present

00:53:48.520 --> 00:53:49.510
a point estimate.

00:53:50.740 --> 00:53:52.420
It's a range between
this and that.

00:53:52.420 --> 00:53:53.710
It's a range between
this and that.

00:53:53.710 --> 00:53:56.770
It's a range between X and
Y. And if you get really fancy,

00:53:56.770 --> 00:53:59.530
you can actually convolve them
all in a simulation and say,

00:53:59.530 --> 00:54:01.570
here is the entire
range of outcomes,

00:54:01.570 --> 00:54:05.140
and this is what it looks like. No,
it's not a bell curve. It never is.

00:54:05.140 --> 00:54:06.145
RACHAEL: Yeah.

00:54:06.145 --> 00:54:07.585
- But so

00:54:08.800 --> 00:54:10.300
when you talk about ROI,

00:54:10.300 --> 00:54:11.800
that's how I've always
thought about it.

00:54:11.800 --> 00:54:13.960
That's how I've always been
forced to present it though.

00:54:13.960 --> 00:54:15.565
Because if I'm asking

00:54:17.060 --> 00:54:21.560
my division leader, president, whoever
to spend 50, 75, or 100

00:54:21.560 --> 00:54:24.230
grand, or to dedicate--

00:54:24.230 --> 00:54:28.460
you think about a man, a person
hours, 2,000 hours a year,

00:54:28.460 --> 00:54:31.070
maybe I want to dedicate 1,000
hours of the day to scientists

00:54:31.070 --> 00:54:32.780
that cost me X amount of money.

00:54:32.780 --> 00:54:34.310
What am I going
to get for that?

00:54:35.690 --> 00:54:36.680
And that's just

00:54:37.430 --> 00:54:40.550
some of the ways that I've had
to present it and pitch it and

00:54:40.550 --> 00:54:43.130
get people to sign off and
say yes, we will give you the

00:54:43.130 --> 00:54:45.710
money, or we will give
you the time to do that.

00:54:47.330 --> 00:54:49.850
And to the earlier point,
it's all about what metrics.

00:54:49.850 --> 00:54:51.260
What are you going to improve?

00:54:51.260 --> 00:54:54.470
You're either going to improve
revenue or cut costs or improve

00:54:54.470 --> 00:54:55.805
efficiency with cut cost.

00:54:57.210 --> 00:55:02.010
You have to have that
in mind. Otherwise, it's just R&amp;amp;D.

00:55:02.010 --> 00:55:04.350
And if it's just R&amp;amp;D, then,

00:55:04.350 --> 00:55:06.930
you got to be able to
pitch that to, hey, this is R&amp;amp;D.

00:55:06.930 --> 00:55:08.130
We don't have anything for it.

00:55:09.110 --> 00:55:11.450
But we aren't going into
space that we've never been before.

00:55:11.450 --> 00:55:14.405
And sometimes, you got
to be honest with that.

00:55:14.405 --> 00:55:18.620
- Yeah, well, maybe while
we're on the insurance example here, I want

00:55:18.620 --> 00:55:22.340
to jump over to Adam's question
in the chat.

00:55:22.340 --> 00:55:24.200
If you're still on, Adam.

00:55:24.200 --> 00:55:26.195
- Oh, yeah, this could
be a real rabbit hole.

00:55:26.760 --> 00:55:29.480
So we're almost at time.
I don't want to keep anybody.

00:55:29.480 --> 00:55:31.880
But yeah, I think the question
is we were talking earlier about

00:55:32.570 --> 00:55:34.700
this idea, false positives
and true positives.

00:55:34.700 --> 00:55:36.410
And like in a
classification problem,

00:55:36.410 --> 00:55:37.610
you can kind of tune that,

00:55:37.610 --> 00:55:40.595
and you kind of associate costs
and things to those various outcomes.

00:55:42.820 --> 00:55:45.040
Yeah, so in insurance, I'm
in property and casualty

00:55:45.040 --> 00:55:46.585
insurance. And we work on

00:55:47.440 --> 00:55:48.760
models that go into pricing,

00:55:48.760 --> 00:55:51.970
and it's way more complicated
than just sort of true and

00:55:51.970 --> 00:55:54.760
false positives. We
build a pricing model.

00:55:54.760 --> 00:55:58.600
We try to estimate losses
for a given set of features.

00:55:58.600 --> 00:56:01.810
And then what happens is
that all that information kind of

00:56:01.810 --> 00:56:03.655
goes over the wall to
actuarial partners.

00:56:05.110 --> 00:56:07.060
Our product partners who have
certain kinds of business

00:56:07.060 --> 00:56:08.260
objectives, they have to hit.

00:56:08.260 --> 00:56:12.520
So, actually, the models kind
of get tweaked and modified.

00:56:12.520 --> 00:56:14.050
And then they layer
in additional things like

00:56:14.050 --> 00:56:17.050
discounts and so forth. And so
when I go in thinking about,

00:56:17.050 --> 00:56:18.760
OK, I want to
improve these models.

00:56:18.760 --> 00:56:21.680
Maybe I'm bringing in a new
third party data source, for example.

00:56:21.680 --> 00:56:24.910
I want to figure out if this
is a worthwhile data source to include.

00:56:27.070 --> 00:56:28.630
I have to figure out
is the model improving?

00:56:28.630 --> 00:56:30.565
But then I have to know
also is it worth buying?

00:56:31.750 --> 00:56:35.320
And so the model is kind of
decoupled in some ways from the

00:56:35.320 --> 00:56:38.290
final outcomes in
really complex ways.

00:56:38.290 --> 00:56:40.990
And I'm wondering if there's
some way to think about this

00:56:40.990 --> 00:56:43.880
fuzzy sort of connection
between model improvement and

00:56:44.600 --> 00:56:47.810
ROI at the end of the
day. It's a long winded explanation, but--

00:56:47.810 --> 00:56:48.310
- No,

00:56:49.010 --> 00:56:52.580
I think a lot of decisions
get made on overly simple metrics

00:56:52.580 --> 00:56:55.130
just because it's
so overwhelming. Like

00:56:56.620 --> 00:56:59.770
not all customers produce
the same amount of value,

00:56:59.770 --> 00:57:02.980
but so many business decisions
are based exclusively on

00:57:02.980 --> 00:57:05.140
conversion and retention as
though every customer were

00:57:05.140 --> 00:57:05.875
equally valuable.

00:57:06.950 --> 00:57:07.730
So but

00:57:08.670 --> 00:57:11.250
you're going to overwhelm
your stakeholders if you're simultaneously

00:57:12.060 --> 00:57:15.090
introducing a new justification
for like, oh, well,

00:57:15.660 --> 00:57:18.710
deduct these costs, add these
lifetime value benefits, et cetera.

00:57:18.710 --> 00:57:19.020
DAN: Exactly.

00:57:19.020 --> 00:57:20.790
- Those have to become
separate issues.

00:57:21.450 --> 00:57:21.990
So then,

00:57:21.990 --> 00:57:24.660
you just have this reliable
lifetime value metric you could

00:57:24.660 --> 00:57:26.950
pull off the shelf. And
you could ask now, hey,

00:57:26.950 --> 00:57:30.780
what if we made decisions
based on three-year revenue per

00:57:30.780 --> 00:57:33.570
customer rather
than raw conversion,

00:57:33.570 --> 00:57:35.220
which treats everyone
as equally valuable?

00:57:36.410 --> 00:57:39.470
How much more money
would we save?

00:57:39.470 --> 00:57:40.730
How much more money
would we make?

00:57:42.960 --> 00:57:45.570
Those are all kind of
your ways to shape ROI,

00:57:45.570 --> 00:57:47.410
by redefining the
question. I don't know,

00:57:47.410 --> 00:57:49.110
maybe that was kind of a
theme today was actually just like,

00:57:49.110 --> 00:57:51.360
you are in a very
powerful position to redefine the

00:57:51.360 --> 00:57:52.800
question when
you're doing an ROI

00:57:54.390 --> 00:57:56.955
investigation and don't
take that lightly.

00:57:57.660 --> 00:57:58.160
But

00:57:58.590 --> 00:58:00.730
keep in mind that people
only have so much attention.

00:58:00.730 --> 00:58:04.530
So you got to modularize things
so that they can stay on board.

00:58:04.530 --> 00:58:07.220
I see so many data
science projects just completely overwhelm

00:58:08.760 --> 00:58:12.120
their stakeholders.
So avoid that, and

00:58:13.020 --> 00:58:15.420
yeah, you can get a
lot of influence from that, so.

00:58:15.420 --> 00:58:17.430
- Jerod, I know you
had your hand raised.

00:58:17.430 --> 00:58:19.020
So I know we're
right at time here,

00:58:19.020 --> 00:58:21.975
but just wanted to see if
you had time to ask your question.

00:58:22.725 --> 00:58:26.760
JEROD: Oh, I just wanted
to make a comment about these

00:58:26.760 --> 00:58:30.420
moonshot ideas and
these R&amp;amp;D ideas.

00:58:31.650 --> 00:58:32.505
We usually

00:58:33.270 --> 00:58:35.880
at the beginning of the year
just agree upon an amount of

00:58:35.880 --> 00:58:37.635
time that you have
to do research

00:58:39.750 --> 00:58:43.913
for projects that don't
necessarily have an immediate impact.

00:58:44.600 --> 00:58:45.100
And

00:58:45.640 --> 00:58:47.800
then basically,
throughout the year,

00:58:47.800 --> 00:58:51.190
if you come across a new really
cool thing that you want to work on,

00:58:52.180 --> 00:58:52.680
the

00:58:53.140 --> 00:58:55.270
question immediately
bounces back to you.

00:58:55.270 --> 00:58:57.700
Is this worth stopping
the other research that you're

00:58:57.700 --> 00:59:00.700
doing right now and
switch to the new project?

00:59:00.700 --> 00:59:02.830
Can you wrap it
up can you switch?

00:59:02.830 --> 00:59:05.080
Because you only
have so much time.

00:59:05.080 --> 00:59:09.340
And then there is not
even a need to generate

00:59:10.510 --> 00:59:12.250
a return on that investment.

00:59:12.250 --> 00:59:13.890
And then,

00:59:15.010 --> 00:59:15.510
usually,

00:59:16.150 --> 00:59:17.860
I can, myself,

00:59:17.860 --> 00:59:20.200
answer the question whether
I want to switch or not.

00:59:20.200 --> 00:59:23.050
Because I either don't want to
give up on the project that I'm

00:59:23.050 --> 00:59:26.515
working on, or I'm done
with the previous project.

00:59:28.750 --> 00:59:29.620
Hope that helps them.

00:59:30.560 --> 00:59:35.840
- Well, I don't want to
keep you, Derek and Joe, too long.

00:59:35.840 --> 00:59:40.240
So thank you so much for
sharing your insights and experience here.

00:59:40.240 --> 00:59:42.640
I just want to see if you
had anything else you wanted to add

00:59:42.640 --> 00:59:44.440
to the conversation
before we go.

00:59:46.350 --> 00:59:47.155
- It's hard.

00:59:47.920 --> 00:59:49.315
Good luck, everyone.

